  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Kenilworth</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Kenilworth</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Terracotta Joint Failure and Cracking</h3>
          <p>Terracotta cornices and entry features crack as mortar shrinks and the material thermally expands. Standard brick mortar is not appropriate — a soft, flexible formulation specific to terracotta's characteristics is required.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Lime Mortar Shrinkage on Pre-Depression Facades</h3>
          <p>90–130-year-old lime putty has shrunk and recessed, letting water and frost infiltrate. Repair requires lime-based mortar with period-appropriate aggregate — specialist tuckpointing, not standard residential practice.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Lakefront Moisture Efflorescence</h3>
          <p>Lake Michigan moisture drives efflorescence across east and north elevations — salt deposits indicating sustained water infiltration. Surface treatment alone won't fix it; comprehensive tuckpointing is required.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Limestone Sill and Coping Joint Deterioration</h3>
          <p>Open joints on limestone sills and copings allow water infiltration that causes both stone deterioration and structural damage behind the facade. Limestone repointing requires softer, lime-based mortar than adjacent brick.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

